## The binomial model: counting successes
Use the **binomial distribution** for the number of successes $X$ when four conditions hold (remember **BINS**):

- **B**inary: each trial is success or failure;
- **I**ndependent trials;
- **N**umber of trials $n$ fixed in advance;
- **S**ame success probability $p$ on every trial.

Then
$$P(X = k) = \binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k}, \qquad \mu = np, \qquad \sigma = \sqrt{np(1-p)}$$
$\binom{n}{k}$ ("$n$ choose $k$") counts the orders in which $k$ successes can appear among $n$ trials.
